WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:02.961
[upbeat music]

00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:10.594
Wong: Hi, I'm Stephanie Wong,
and I work for Google Cloud.

00:00:10.594 --> 00:00:13.222
While I could talk all day
about cloud security,

00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:15.390
physical security
at a Google data center

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.808
is still pretty new to me,

00:00:16.808 --> 00:00:19.436
so today I'm on a mission
to learn all about it

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:22.231
by taking an inside look
at the systems in place

00:00:22.231 --> 00:00:23.649
that protect customer data

00:00:23.649 --> 00:00:25.943
at a typical Google data center.
Let's go.

00:00:25.943 --> 00:00:28.695
[upbeat music]

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.949
Now, I've been told there are six layers
of security here.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.910
Security layer one refers
to the property boundaries,

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.162
and that includes signage and fencing.

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:38.830
But things really start
to get interesting

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.334
when it comes to layer two,
also known as the secure perimeter,

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:46.672
and that includes the main entrance gate
which I am pulling up to right now.

00:00:46.672 --> 00:00:49.091
[upbeat music]

00:00:49.091 --> 00:00:51.510
Hey, how's it going?
person: Good morning.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:57.516
[upbeat music]

00:00:58.934 --> 00:01:01.979
Wong: So layer two has
a lot of security features

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:04.898
ranging from smart fencing
to overlapping cameras

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.901
to 24/7 guard patrols and more.

00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:09.736
I'm on my way to meet some experts

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.655
who are going to show me
how it all works.

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:13.907
Hi, Joe.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:15.075
Kava: Hi, Stephanie, how are you?

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.078
Wong: So I just passed the main gate
and I saw guards and cameras,

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.873
but what are some things
that I didn't see?

00:01:20.873 --> 00:01:22.416
Kava: Yeah there's actually
a lot of technology

00:01:22.416 --> 00:01:24.168
and operations
going on behind the scene.

00:01:24.168 --> 00:01:26.086
So from the time that you're on site,

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:27.462
we know that you're here,

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:30.174
and we're able to do
correlation analysis

00:01:30.174 --> 00:01:31.091
of where you've been.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.887
We have guards in vehicles,
we have some guards on foot.

00:01:34.887 --> 00:01:37.097
There's also the vehicle crash barrier.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:39.099
That's designed to stop
a fully loaded truck

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.143
from crashing
through the front entrance.

00:01:42.603 --> 00:01:45.939
Wong: Ricky, Tarik, can you tell me more
about what's unique about the fencing?

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.483
Gordon: This particular fence
is an anti-climb fence.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:52.070
It's also equipped with fiber.
The technology tells us

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.489
if someone's near the fence
or touches the fence.

00:01:55.407 --> 00:01:57.993
Billingsley: So we use thermal cameras
and standard cameras.

00:01:57.993 --> 00:02:00.078
So we're able to see
video footage at night

00:02:00.078 --> 00:02:01.747
just as clearly as we can
during the day.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:07.377
[light electronic music]

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:10.130
Wong: Welcome to layer three,
building access.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.674
But just so you know,
I am still nowhere near

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:14.134
the data center floor.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.221
That's a few more layers deep.
Let's head inside.

00:02:17.221 --> 00:02:18.180
O'Brien: Stephanie.
Wong: Hello.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.265
O'Brien: So you've gotten through
the gate, you've come in,

00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:21.808
you've come in to our secure lobby.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:24.394
You have your card,
and we know that that's you,

00:02:24.394 --> 00:02:26.772
but if someone happened to lose
their card,

00:02:26.772 --> 00:02:29.149
what we want to make sure is
that it's actually Stephanie

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.192
who has shown up.

00:02:30.192 --> 00:02:32.194
scanner: Please center your eye.

00:02:32.194 --> 00:02:34.238
O'Brien: And with iris scan,
we can authenticate

00:02:34.238 --> 00:02:37.699
that it's actually you
along with your ID.

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:39.076
Wong: Okay, I think it's good.

00:02:41.912 --> 00:02:43.163
One thing that's a little
hard to get used to

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.374
when you visit a data center is,

00:02:45.374 --> 00:02:47.543
for secure areas, only one person

00:02:47.543 --> 00:02:51.046
is allowed to badge
through a door at a time.

00:02:51.046 --> 00:02:57.052
[light electronic music]

00:03:02.266 --> 00:03:05.477
Layer four includes
the security operations center,

00:03:05.477 --> 00:03:09.064
or SOC, a hive of activity that
is monitoring the data center

00:03:09.064 --> 00:03:12.234
24/7, 365 days a year.

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:14.444
[light electronic music]

00:03:14.444 --> 00:03:17.239
So it sounds like we've been
keeping them very busy today.

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:19.032
Davis: Yes, yes you have.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:22.035
So the doors, the cameras,
the badge readers,

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.329
the iris scan--
everything is connected here.

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:27.124
This is the brains
of the security system.

00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:30.169
So if there's anything out
of the ordinary happening,

00:03:30.169 --> 00:03:32.337
they have to be able to pick that up.

00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:38.343
[upbeat music]

00:03:40.137 --> 00:03:41.972
Wong: Interesting fact about layer five,

00:03:41.972 --> 00:03:43.390
the data center floor:

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.102
less than 1% of Googlers
ever get to set foot in here.

00:03:47.102 --> 00:03:48.979
So right now, I'm feeling kinda special.

00:03:48.979 --> 00:03:54.985
[upbeat music]

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:02.117
Kava: This is truly
a as-needed only access area,

00:04:02.117 --> 00:04:04.453
meaning that only the technicians

00:04:04.453 --> 00:04:06.872
and engineers that have to be there

00:04:06.872 --> 00:04:09.666
to maintain, upgrade,
or repair the equipment

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:10.792
are ever allowed there.

00:04:12.002 --> 00:04:15.422
Wong: And do Googlers or anyone
have access to the data?

00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:18.884
Kava: We have access to the devices,
but the data at rest

00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:21.386
is encrypted,
and our customers can issue

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:23.472
and keep their own encryption keys,

00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:26.558
and we do this because
protecting the privacy

00:04:26.558 --> 00:04:31.146
and the security of our users' data
is our highest priority.

00:04:31.897 --> 00:04:34.399
Wong: The mysterious layer six,
where disks

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:37.736
are erased and destroyed
and the fewest number of people

00:04:37.736 --> 00:04:39.071
are allowed to enter.

00:04:39.071 --> 00:04:41.907
Drives that need to be retired
from the data center floor

00:04:41.907 --> 00:04:45.327
come into this room
through a secure two-way locker system

00:04:45.327 --> 00:04:48.121
which means that only technicians
assigned to this room

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:50.415
can pull them
from that locker to either erase

00:04:50.415 --> 00:04:52.125
or destroy them.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.503
Henley: All right,
welcome to the crusher room.

00:04:54.503 --> 00:04:55.337
Wong: Wow.

00:04:55.337 --> 00:04:57.589
Henley: So at this point,
we have scanned the hard drive,

00:04:57.589 --> 00:05:00.008
and the software has told us
that we need to destroy it.

00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:01.176
Wong: Can we see it in action?

00:05:01.176 --> 00:05:02.803
Henley: Back up.
Wong: All right.

00:05:02.803 --> 00:05:05.097
I'll stay back here.
[both laugh]

00:05:05.097 --> 00:05:07.057
[mechanical whirring]

00:05:07.599 --> 00:05:09.434
That disk is definitely destroyed.

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:11.687
Henley: Yes it is.

00:05:11.687 --> 00:05:15.649
[upbeat music]

00:05:15.649 --> 00:05:17.401
Wong: If you didn't think
these six layers of security

00:05:17.401 --> 00:05:20.112
were enough,
Google Cloud actually has

00:05:20.112 --> 00:05:22.447
two security testing programs in place.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:24.241
One hires companies to try to break in

00:05:24.241 --> 00:05:26.243
to data center sites from the outside,

00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:29.162
and the other tasks Googlers
with trying to break

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.415
security protocols from the inside.

00:05:33.417 --> 00:05:37.171
And getting out of a data center
is arguably even harder

00:05:37.171 --> 00:05:41.216
than getting in, as everybody
has to go through full metal detection

00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:43.802
each time they leave
the data center floor.

00:05:43.802 --> 00:05:46.513
[upbeat music]

00:05:46.513 --> 00:05:47.389
person: Thank you, ma'am,
for your cooperation.

00:05:47.389 --> 00:05:49.558
Wong: Thank you.

00:05:49.558 --> 00:05:51.435
Google Cloud supports compliance

00:05:51.435 --> 00:05:55.689
with over 40 global standards,
regulations, and certifications,

00:05:55.689 --> 00:05:59.359
and the commitment to constantly test,
optimize, and improve systems

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.528
makes it a leader
in data center security.

00:06:02.946 --> 00:06:04.907
Now, how do I get out of here?

00:06:04.907 --> 00:06:08.493
[upbeat music]

